LM.PAGES = {"pages":{"a1/cases-accusative.md":"---\nid: a1-cases-accusative\nlevel: A1\ncategory: cases\ntitle: Accusative: the direct object\nsummary: The form for the thing or person an action goes to. Mam brata. Lubię kawę.\n---\n\n# Accusative: the direct object\n\nEnglish shows who does what by word order: \"Anna loves Tom\" is not \"Tom loves Anna\". Polish shows it with endings. The person or thing the action goes to (the direct object) takes the **accusative** (*biernik*, questions: *kogo? co?*). *Mam brata. Lubię kawę.* The good news: for many nouns the accusative is the same as the dictionary form.\n\n## How it works\n\nSingular. The plural comes at A2: [[a2-cases-accusative-plural]].\n\n| Gender | Noun | Adjective | Nominative → accusative |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| masculine: people and animals | **-a** | **-ego** | *dobry brat → Mam dobrego brata.* |\n| masculine: things | no change | no change | *nowy telefon → Mam nowy telefon.* |\n| feminine | **-a → -ę** | **-a → -ą** | *dobra kawa → Piję dobrą kawę.* |\n| neuter | no change | no change | *małe piwo → Poproszę małe piwo.* |\n\nSo only two groups really change: masculine nouns for people and animals, and feminine nouns.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **After most verbs that take an object:** *mieć, lubić, znać, czytać, pić, jeść, kupować, oglądać, kochać.* *Czytam książkę.* (I'm reading a book.)\n2. **When you order or ask for something:** *Poproszę kawę i wodę.* (A coffee and a water, please.)\n3. **With days of the week after *w*:** *w środę* (on Wednesday), *w sobotę* (on Saturday).\n4. **Not after *nie*.** A negated object takes the genitive: *Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu.* See [[a1-cases-genitive-basics]].\n\n## Examples\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Mam brata i siostrę. | I have a brother and a sister. |\n| Lubię polską kuchnię. | I like Polish food. |\n| Czytasz książkę czy gazetę? | Are you reading a book or a newspaper? |\n| Poproszę herbatę z cytryną. | A tea with lemon, please. |\n| Anna ma małego psa. | Anna has a small dog. |\n| Kupuję nowy telefon. | I'm buying a new phone. |\n| Jem kanapkę i piję sok. | I'm eating a sandwich and drinking juice. |\n| Znasz mojego kolegę? | Do you know my friend? |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- Masculine nouns that end in **-a** (*kolega, tata, mężczyzna*) take **-ę** like feminine nouns, but their adjectives stay masculine: *Znam twojego tatę.*\n- *pani* becomes *panią*: *Przepraszam panią.* (Excuse me, ma'am.)\n- Some nouns lose an *e* before the ending: *pies → psa*, *ojciec → ojca*.\n\n## Common mistakes for English speakers\n\n- ✗ *Lubię kawa.* → ✓ *Lubię kawę.* — the object needs its ending; word order does not mark it in Polish.\n- ✗ *Mam brat.* → ✓ *Mam brata.* — masculine people and animals take *-a*.\n- ✗ *Piję dobra kawę.* → ✓ *Piję dobrą kawę.* — the adjective changes too: *-a → -ą*.\n- ✗ *Kupuję nowego samochoda.* → ✓ *Kupuję nowy samochód.* — masculine things do not change.\n- ✗ *To jest kawę.* → ✓ *To jest kawa.* — after *to jest* use the nominative.\n\n## Practice\n\nPut the words in brackets into the accusative.\n\n1. Mam (siostra) ___.\n2. Lubisz (herbata) ___?\n3. Poproszę (zupa pomidorowa) ___.\n4. Tomek ma (kot) ___.\n5. Czytam (ciekawa książka) ___.\n6. Mamy (nowy nauczyciel) ___.\n7. Kupuję (mleko) ___ i (chleb) ___.\n\nTranslate into Polish.\n\n8. I have a brother.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. siostrę\n2. herbatę\n3. zupę pomidorową\n4. kota\n5. ciekawą książkę\n6. nowego nauczyciela\n7. mleko, chleb\n8. Mam brata.\n","a1/functions-greetings-introductions.md":"---\nid: a1-functions-greetings-introductions\nlevel: A1\ncategory: functions\ntitle: Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself\nsummary: Dzień dobry or Cześć? Formal and informal greetings, saying your name, Miło mi, and ways to say goodbye.\n---\n\n# Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself\n\nEnglish uses \"hello\" with almost everyone. Polish makes a clear choice: *Dzień dobry* (formal) for strangers, shops, offices and older people, *Cześć* (informal) for friends, family and children. Choosing the wrong one is the first thing people notice, so learn the pairs together. To say your name, Polish does not say \"my name is\": it says *Mam na imię Anna* (\"I have the name Anna\") or simply *Jestem Anna*.\n\n## Key phrases\n\nSaying hello\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Dzień dobry. | Good morning. / Good afternoon. / Hello. (formal, until evening) |\n| Dobry wieczór. | Good evening. (formal) |\n| Cześć! | Hi! (informal) |\n| Hej! | Hey! (informal) |\n\nSaying who you are\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Mam na imię Anna. | My name is Anna. (first name) |\n| Nazywam się Anna Nowak. | My name is Anna Nowak. (full name, formal) |\n| Jestem Tom. | I'm Tom. |\n| Jak masz na imię? | What's your name? (informal) |\n| Jak się pan nazywa? / Jak się pani nazywa? | What's your name? (formal, to a man / to a woman) |\n| Miło mi. / Bardzo mi miło. | Nice to meet you. |\n\nAsking how someone is\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Jak się masz? | How are you? (informal) |\n| Jak się pan ma? / Jak się pani ma? | How are you? (formal, to a man / to a woman) |\n| Dobrze, dziękuję. A ty? | Fine, thanks. And you? (informal) |\n| Dobrze, dziękuję. A pan? / A pani? | Fine, thank you. And you? (formal) |\n| W porządku. | All right. / OK. |\n\nSaying goodbye\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Do widzenia. | Goodbye. (formal) |\n| Cześć! / Na razie! | Bye! / See you! (informal) |\n| Do zobaczenia. | See you. (neutral, also formal) |\n| Do jutra! | See you tomorrow! |\n| Dobranoc. | Good night. (late, when you leave or go to bed) |\n\n*Cześć* works both ways: hello and goodbye.\n\n## Dialogue\n\nAnna meets her neighbour, pan Adam, outside their block of flats. Her friend Piotr arrives.\n\n> **Anna:** Dzień dobry! (Hello!)\n> **Pan Adam:** Dzień dobry! Jak się pani ma? (Hello! How are you?)\n> **Anna:** Dobrze, dziękuję. A pan? (Fine, thank you. And you?)\n> **Pan Adam:** W porządku, dziękuję. (All right, thank you.)\n> **Piotr:** Cześć, Ania! (Hi, Ania!)\n> **Anna:** Cześć, Piotrek! To jest pan Adam Nowak. (Hi, Piotrek! This is Mr Adam Nowak.)\n> **Piotr:** Dzień dobry. Nazywam się Piotr Wójcik. Miło mi. (Hello. My name is Piotr Wójcik. Nice to meet you.)\n> **Pan Adam:** Bardzo mi miło. Do widzenia! (Very nice to meet you. Goodbye!)\n> **Anna:** Do widzenia! (Goodbye!)\n\nAnna and Piotr are friends, so they say *Cześć*. With pan Adam both use *Dzień dobry* and *Do widzenia*.\n\n## Culture note\n\n- Say *Dzień dobry* when you walk into a small shop, a doctor's waiting room or a lift, and *Do widzenia* when you leave.\n- Neighbours and colleagues often use *pan* or *pani* with a first name: *pan Adam*, *pani Ewa*. It is polite but friendly.\n- Friends use short forms of names: *Anna → Ania, Anka*; *Piotr → Piotrek*; *Katarzyna → Kasia*.\n- People usually shake hands when they meet for the first time.\n\n## Common mistakes for English speakers\n\n- ✗ *Dobry dzień!* → ✓ *Dzień dobry!* — a fixed phrase; the word order is not the English \"good day\".\n- ✗ *Moje imię jest Tom.* → ✓ *Mam na imię Tom.* — Polish says \"I have the name\", or just *Jestem Tom*.\n- ✗ *Jak się jesteś?* → ✓ *Jak się masz?* — the phrase uses *masz* (\"have\"), not \"are\".\n- ✗ *Cześć!* to a shop assistant → ✓ *Dzień dobry!* — *cześć* is for friends, family and people your age in informal settings.\n- ✗ *Dziekuje.* → ✓ *Dziękuję.* — the marks are part of the spelling.\n\n## Practice\n\nFormal or informal? Choose the right greeting.\n\n1. You meet your friend Kasia: *Dzień dobry* or *Cześć*?\n2. You walk into a bakery: *Dzień dobry* or *Cześć*?\n3. You leave a doctor's office: *Do widzenia* or *Na razie*?\n\nComplete the phrase.\n\n4. Mam ___ imię Marek. (My name is Marek.)\n5. Bardzo mi ___. (Very nice to meet you.)\n\nTranslate into Polish.\n\n6. How are you? (to a friend)\n7. How are you? (formal, to a woman)\n8. Good evening. (formal)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. Cześć\n2. Dzień dobry\n3. Do widzenia\n4. na\n5. miło\n6. Jak się masz?\n7. Jak się pani ma?\n8. Dobry wieczór.\n","a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Alphabet, sounds and stress\nsummary: Polish letters and sounds, the letters with marks, and the stress rule: second-to-last syllable. Dzień dobry. Dziękuję.\n---\n\n# Alphabet, sounds and stress\n\nPolish spelling is far more regular than English: know the letters and you can read any word aloud. There are no silent letters, and the stress almost always falls on the **second-to-last syllable**. New for English speakers: nine letters with marks (*ą, ć, ę, ł, ń, ó, ś, ź, ż*) and letter pairs that make one sound (*sz, cz, rz, ch, dz, dż*).\n\n## How it works\n\n32 letters (*q, v, x* only in foreign words):\n\n*a ą b c ć d e ę f g h i j k l ł m n ń o ó p r s ś t u w y z ź ż*\n\nLetters that surprise English speakers:\n\n| Letter | Sounds like | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| *c* | \"ts\" in cats | *noc* (night) |\n| *ch*, *h* | \"ch\" in Scottish loch | *chleb* (bread), *herbata* (tea) |\n| *cz* | \"ch\" in church | *czas* (time) |\n| *sz* | \"sh\" in shop | *szkoła* (school) |\n| ***rz***, ***ż*** | \"s\" in pleasure | *rzeka* (river), *żona* (wife) |\n| *j* | \"y\" in yes | *jajko* (egg) |\n| *w* | \"v\" in van | *woda* (water) |\n| *ł* | \"w\" in water | *mały* (small) |\n| *y* | \"i\" in bit | *ty* (you) |\n| ***ó***, ***u*** | \"oo\" in book | *ołówek* (pencil), *ucho* (ear) |\n| *ą* | nasal \"on\", as in French bon | *są* (they are) |\n| *ę* | nasal \"en\"; at the end of a word almost a plain \"e\" | *ręka* (hand), *dziękuję* (thank you) |\n\n**Soft sounds** have two spellings: a mark before a consonant or at the end of a word, an *i* before a vowel. If the vowel is *i* itself, just write it: *zima* (winter), *nic* (nothing).\n\n| Mark | Before a vowel | Sounds like | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| *ś* | *si* | a soft \"sh\" (tongue flat, as in sheep) | *środa*, *siostra* |\n| *ć* | *ci* | a soft \"ch\" (as in cheese) | *cześć*, *ciocia* |\n| *ź* | *zi* | a soft \"zh\" | *źle*, *zima* |\n| *dź* | *dzi* | a soft \"j\" (as in jeep) | *dzień* |\n| *ń* | *ni* | \"ny\" in canyon | *koń*, *nie* |\n\n**Stress:** the second-to-last syllable. An *i* before a vowel is not a syllable of its own: *Ka-sia* has two.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Reading a new word:** say every letter or letter pair; an *i* before a vowel only softens the sound before it. *Kraków* = *Kra-ków*.\n2. **Stress:** count syllables from the end. *War-sza-wa* → war-**SZA**-wa (Warsaw).\n3. **Marks change words:** *sad* (orchard) and *sąd* (court) are different words. *rz / ż*, *ó / u* and *ch / h* sound the same, so learn each word with its spelling.\n\n## Examples\n\n| Polish | English | Stress |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Dzień dobry. | Good morning. / Hello. | *dzień DO-bry* |\n| Dziękuję. | Thank you. | *dzię-KU-ję* |\n| Przepraszam. | Excuse me. / Sorry. | *prze-PRA-szam* |\n| Warszawa | Warsaw | *war-SZA-wa* |\n| herbata | tea | *her-BA-ta* |\n| poniedziałek | Monday | *po-nie-DZIA-łek* |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- At the end of a word *b, d, g, w, z* sound like *p, t, k, f, s*: *chleb* sounds like \"chlep\", *Kraków* like \"Krakuf\".\n- After *p, t, k, ch* the pair *rz* sounds like *sz*: *przepraszam*, *trzy* (three).\n\n## Common mistakes for English speakers\n\n- ✗ *Lodz* → ✓ *Łódź* — *Lodz* is the English spelling; in Polish the marks are part of the letters.\n- ✗ *woda* said with an English \"w\" → ✓ *woda* with a \"v\" — Polish *w* is a v sound (an f at the end of a word).\n- ✗ *WAR-sza-wa* → ✓ *war-SZA-wa* — stress the second-to-last syllable, not the first.\n- ✗ *rzeka* said as \"r\" + \"z\" → ✓ *rzeka* with the \"s\" of *pleasure* — *rz* is one sound, the same as *ż*.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the stressed syllable in capitals.\n\n1. *te-le-fon*\n2. *her-ba-ta*\n3. *ro-wer*\n\nWhich spelling makes the same sound?\n\n4. *ż* sounds the same as ___.\n5. *ó* sounds the same as ___.\n\nAdd the missing marks.\n\n6. *Dzien dobry.*\n7. *Przepraszam, ktora godzina?* (Excuse me, what time is it?)\n\nHow do you say it?\n\n8. The *w* in *woda* sounds like English ___.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. te-LE-fon\n2. her-BA-ta\n3. RO-wer\n4. rz\n5. u\n6. Dzień dobry.\n7. Przepraszam, która godzina?\n8. v\n","a1/grammar-pronouns-pan-pani.md":"---\nid: a1-grammar-pronouns-pan-pani\nlevel: A1\ncategory: grammar\ntitle: Personal pronouns and formal address\nsummary: ja, ty, on, ona, ono, my, wy, oni, one, and the polite pan, pani, państwo for \"you\". Jak się pan ma?\n---\n\n# Personal pronouns and formal address\n\nEnglish has one \"you\" for everyone. Polish has *ty* (one person) and *wy* (several people) for friends, family and children, and *pan* (to a man), *pani* (to a woman), *państwo* (to a group) for everyone else. The polite words really mean \"sir\", \"madam\" and \"ladies and gentlemen\", so the verb after them is the \"he / she / they\" form. Polish also has two words for \"they\", and often leaves pronouns out.\n\n## How it works\n\n| Person | Singular | Plural |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1st | *ja* (I) | *my* (we) |\n| 2nd, informal | *ty* (you, one friend) | *wy* (you, several friends) |\n| 3rd | *on* (he), *ona* (she), ***ono*** (it) | ***oni*** (they: a group with at least one man), ***one*** (they: all other groups) |\n\nFormal \"you\":\n\n| Talking to | Polish | Verb form |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a man | *pan* | like *on*: *Jak się pan ma?* |\n| a woman | *pani* | like *ona*: *Jak się pani ma?* |\n| a mixed group, or a couple | ***państwo*** | like *oni*: *Jak się państwo mają?* |\n| a group of men / of women | *panowie* / *panie* | like *oni* / *one* |\n\n*ono* is for neuter nouns, for example *dziecko* (child). Things are usually *on* or *ona*: a phone (*telefon*) is *on*, a lamp (*lampa*) is *ona*: see [[a1-grammar-gender-of-nouns]].\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Usually leave the pronoun out.** The verb ending already says who: *Jak się masz?* (How are you?), not *Jak się ty masz?*\n2. **Use it for contrast or emphasis.** *Ja jestem Ewa, a ty?* (I'm Ewa, and you?)\n3. **Use *ty* with friends, family and children.** *Cześć, Ola! Jak się masz?* (Hi, Ola! How are you?)\n4. **Use *pan / pani* with strangers, in shops, at work and with older people.** *Dzień dobry. Jak się pani ma?* (Hello. How are you, madam?)\n5. **Use *oni* if the group has at least one man, *one* if it has none.** *Ewa i Marek? Oni są tu.* (Ewa and Marek? They're here.)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Jak się pan ma? | How are you? (to a man) |\n| Dobrze, dziękuję. A pani? | Fine, thank you. And you? (to a woman) |\n| Dzień dobry! Jak się państwo mają? | Hello! How are you? (formal, to a couple or a group) |\n| Cześć! Jak się macie? | Hi! How are you? (to several friends) |\n| Kasia? Ona jest tu. | Kasia? She's here. |\n| Ania i Ola? One są tam. | Ania and Ola? They're over there. |\n| Piotr i Ewa? Oni są w domu. | Piotr and Ewa? They're at home. |\n| My jesteśmy z Gdańska, a wy? | We're from Gdańsk, and you? |\n\n*jest* (is) and *są* (are) come from *być* (to be): [[a1-verbs-byc]].\n\n## Good to know\n\n- *Proszę pana* / *proszę pani* is how you politely get someone's attention: \"Excuse me, sir / madam\".\n- Moving from *pan / pani* to *ty* is a social step; usually the older person offers it.\n\n## Common mistakes for English speakers\n\n- ✗ *Jak się pan masz?* → ✓ *Jak się pan ma?* — *pan* takes the \"he\" form of the verb, not the \"you\" form.\n- ✗ *Jak się masz?* to a stranger → ✓ *Jak się pan ma?* / *Jak się pani ma?* — *ty* to strangers sounds rude or too familiar.\n- ✗ *Jak się macie?* to one older person → ✓ *Jak się pani ma?* — *wy* is never the polite form for one person.\n- ✗ *Anna i Ewa? Oni są tu.* → ✓ *Anna i Ewa? One są tu.* — *oni* needs at least one man in the group.\n- ✗ *Ja jestem Tom. Ja jestem tu.* → ✓ *Jestem Tom. Jestem tu.* — the verb shows the person; repeating *ja* sounds heavy.\n\n## Practice\n\nWhich pronoun? Replace the name or names.\n\n1. Marek → ___\n2. Kasia i Ola → ___\n3. Kasia i Marek → ___\n4. dziecko → ___\n\nFormal or informal? Choose.\n\n5. To a friend: *A ty?* or *A pan?*\n6. To an older woman you don't know: *Jak się masz?* or *Jak się pani ma?*\n\nTranslate into Polish.\n\n7. How are you? (to a group of friends)\n8. And you? (formal, to a man)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. on\n2. one\n3. oni\n4. ono\n5. A ty?\n6. Jak się pani ma?\n7. Jak się macie?\n8. A pan?\n","a1/verbs-byc.md":"---\nid: a1-verbs-byc\nlevel: A1\ncategory: verbs\ntitle: być (to be) and to jest / to są\nsummary: The forms of być (jestem, jesteś, jest, jesteśmy, jesteście, są) and pointing at things with to jest and to są.\n---\n\n# być (to be) and to jest / to są\n\n*Być* is \"to be\". Like English \"am / is / are\", it is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart. Two things are different from English: the ending already says who, so *Jestem Tom* is a full sentence without \"I\", and \"this is / these are\" is *to jest / to są*, where *to* never changes, whatever you point at.\n\n## How it works\n\nPresent tense of *być*:\n\n| Person | Singular | Plural |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1st | (*ja*) ***jestem*** (I am) | (*my*) ***jesteśmy*** (we are) |\n| 2nd | (*ty*) ***jesteś*** (you are) | (*wy*) ***jesteście*** (you are) |\n| 3rd | (*on, ona, ono*) ***jest*** (he / she / it is) | (*oni, one*) ***są*** (they are) |\n| formal | *pan / pani* ***jest*** | *państwo* ***są*** |\n\nPointing:\n\n| Polish | English | Rule |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| *To jest Marek.* | This is Marek. | one person or thing: *to jest* |\n| *To są moje dzieci.* | These are my children. | more than one: *to są* |\n| *Kto to jest?* | Who is this? | people and animals |\n| *Co to jest?* | What is this? | things |\n\nNegative: put *nie* in front: *nie jestem*, *to nie jest*.\n\n## When to use it\n\n1. **Saying who you are:** *Jestem Ola.* (I'm Ola.)\n2. **Describing:** *Jesteś bardzo miły.* (You're very kind. — to a man; to a woman: *miła*.)\n3. **Saying where someone is:** *Gdzie jesteś? — Jestem tu.* (Where are you? — I'm here.)\n4. **Introducing and pointing:** *To jest pani Nowak.* (This is Mrs Nowak.) *To są moje siostry.* (These are my sisters.)\n5. **Asking:** *Czy to jest twój telefon?* (Is this your phone?) — *czy* at the start turns a statement into a yes / no question.\n\n## Examples\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Jestem Kuba, a to jest Zosia. | I'm Kuba, and this is Zosia. |\n| Gdzie jesteście? — Jesteśmy tu! | Where are you? — We're here! |\n| Pan jest bardzo miły. | You are very kind. (to a man) |\n| Kasia i Marek są tam. | Kasia and Marek are over there. |\n| Co to jest? — To jest herbata. | What's this? — It's tea. |\n| To są moje dzieci, Antek i Zuzia. | These are my children, Antek and Zuzia. |\n| Nie jestem zmęczona. | I'm not tired. (a woman speaking) |\n| Kto to jest? — To jest mój brat. | Who's this? — It's my brother. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- The adjective after *jestem* shows your gender: a man says *jestem zmęczony*, a woman *jestem zmęczona*.\n- For a job or nationality (*I'm a student*, *she's a doctor*) the noun needs a new ending after a form of *być* (*jestem, jest…*): [[a1-cases-instrumental-identity]]. After *to jest* the noun does not change: *To jest student.*\n- Age uses *mieć* (to have), not *być*: *Mam trzydzieści lat* (I'm thirty). See [[a1-functions-personal-details]].\n- *Tu* and *tutaj* both mean \"here\"; *tam* is \"there\".\n\n## Common mistakes for English speakers\n\n- ✗ *Oni jest tu.* → ✓ *Oni są tu.* — the plural \"they are\" is *są*.\n- ✗ *To są mój brat.* → ✓ *To jest mój brat.* — one person: *to jest*.\n- ✗ *To jest moje dzieci.* → ✓ *To są moje dzieci.* — *dzieci* is plural, so *to są*.\n- ✗ *Jestem trzydzieści.* → ✓ *Mam trzydzieści lat.* — age uses *mieć*, not *być*.\n- ✗ *Ja jestem Tom, ja jestem tu.* → ✓ *Jestem Tom, jestem tu.* — *jestem* already means \"I am\".\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete with the right form of *być*.\n\n1. My ___ tu.\n2. Ty ___ Ola?\n3. Oni ___ w domu.\n4. Pan ___ bardzo miły.\n5. Wy ___ tam?\n\n*To jest* or *to są*?\n\n6. ___ moja siostra.\n7. ___ moje dzieci.\n\nTranslate into Polish.\n\n8. Where are you? (to one friend)\n\n## Answers\n\n1. jesteśmy\n2. jesteś\n3. są\n4. jest\n5. jesteście\n6. To jest\n7. To są\n8. Gdzie jesteś?\n","a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months.md":"---\nid: a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months\nlevel: A1\ncategory: vocabulary\ntitle: Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months\nsummary: Count from zero to a hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\". Days and months take small letters.\n---\n\n# Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months\n\nPolish numbers are built like English ones: *dwadzieścia* (twenty) + *dwa* (two) = *dwadzieścia dwa* (twenty-two), with no \"and\" and no hyphen. Two differences matter from day one: days and months are written with a **small letter**, and \"on Monday\" or \"in July\" change the end of the word: *poniedziałek → w poniedziałek*, *lipiec → w lipcu*. Learn these short phrases as they are; the rules behind them come later.\n\n## Words\n\nNumbers 0–20\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| zero | 0 |\n| jeden | 1 |\n| dwa | 2 |\n| trzy | 3 |\n| cztery | 4 |\n| pięć | 5 |\n| sześć | 6 |\n| siedem | 7 |\n| osiem | 8 |\n| dziewięć | 9 |\n| dziesięć | 10 |\n| jedenaście | 11 |\n| dwanaście | 12 |\n| trzynaście | 13 |\n| czternaście | 14 |\n| piętnaście | 15 |\n| szesnaście | 16 |\n| siedemnaście | 17 |\n| osiemnaście | 18 |\n| dziewiętnaście | 19 |\n| dwadzieścia | 20 |\n\nTens and a hundred\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| trzydzieści | 30 |\n| czterdzieści | 40 |\n| pięćdziesiąt | 50 |\n| sześćdziesiąt | 60 |\n| siedemdziesiąt | 70 |\n| osiemdziesiąt | 80 |\n| dziewięćdziesiąt | 90 |\n| sto | 100 |\n\nDays of the week\n\n| Polish | English | on … |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| poniedziałek (m) | Monday | *w poniedziałek* |\n| wtorek (m) | Tuesday | ***we*** *wtorek* |\n| środa (f) | Wednesday | *w środę* |\n| czwartek (m) | Thursday | *w czwartek* |\n| piątek (m) | Friday | *w piątek* |\n| sobota (f) | Saturday | *w sobotę* |\n| niedziela (f) | Sunday | *w niedzielę* |\n\nMonths\n\n| Polish | English | in … |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| styczeń (m) | January | *w styczniu* |\n| luty (m) | February | *w lutym* |\n| marzec (m) | March | *w marcu* |\n| kwiecień (m) | April | *w kwietniu* |\n| maj (m) | May | *w maju* |\n| czerwiec (m) | June | *w czerwcu* |\n| lipiec (m) | July | *w lipcu* |\n| sierpień (m) | August | *w sierpniu* |\n| wrzesień (m) | September | *we wrześniu* |\n| październik (m) | October | *w październiku* |\n| listopad (m) | November | *w listopadzie* |\n| grudzień (m) | December | *w grudniu* |\n\n## Word partners\n\n| Polish | English |\n| --- | --- |\n| Jaki dzisiaj jest dzień? | What day is it today? |\n| Dzisiaj jest środa. | Today is Wednesday. |\n| Jutro jest sobota. | Tomorrow is Saturday. |\n| w sobotę i w niedzielę | on Saturday and Sunday |\n| w weekend | at the weekend |\n| we wtorek rano | on Tuesday morning |\n| w lipcu i w sierpniu | in July and August |\n| Mój numer to sześć, zero, jeden… | My number is six, zero, one… |\n| autobus numer dwadzieścia trzy | bus number twenty-three |\n| pokój numer czternaście | room fourteen |\n| Ile? — Dwanaście. | How many? — Twelve. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n- Phone numbers can be read digit by digit: *sześć, dwa, zero…* Poles often read them in groups of three, which needs the hundreds (A2).\n- *we* instead of *w* before *w* or *f* followed by another consonant: *we wtorek*, *we wrześniu*. It is easier to say.\n- *Jeden* changes with gender (*jedna kawa*, *jedno piwo*), and *dwa* becomes *dwie* with feminine nouns (*dwie kawy*). Counting things comes in [[a1-functions-ordering-shopping]].\n\n## Common mistakes for English speakers\n\n- ✗ *w Poniedziałek* → ✓ *w poniedziałek* — days and months take a small letter in Polish.\n- ✗ *w sobota* → ✓ *w sobotę* — \"on\" + a day changes the ending: *-a → -ę*.\n- ✗ *pięćnaście* → ✓ *piętnaście* — 15 and 19 are spelled with *t*: *piętnaście*, *dziewiętnaście*.\n- ✗ *dwadzieścia i dwa* → ✓ *dwadzieścia dwa* — no \"and\" inside a number.\n- ✗ *w lipiec* → ✓ *w lipcu* — \"in\" + a month changes the ending; learn the *w …* forms as phrases.\n\n## Practice\n\nWrite the numbers in words.\n\n1. 17\n2. 45\n3. 99\n\nWhich day comes next?\n\n4. poniedziałek, wtorek, ___\n5. piątek, sobota, ___\n\nComplete with the right phrase.\n\n6. \"on Saturday\" = w ___\n7. \"in May\" = w ___\n\nTranslate into Polish.\n\n8. Today is Thursday.\n\n## Answers\n\n1. siedemnaście\n2. czterdzieści pięć\n3. dziewięćdziesiąt dziewięć\n4. środa\n5. niedziela\n6. sobotę\n7. maju\n8. Dzisiaj jest czwartek. / Dziś jest czwartek.\n"},"summaries":{},"cheatsheets":{},"home":"# Polish Levels Map\n\nA free map of Polish from the first words to fluency, in six levels from A1 to C2. Every topic is explained in plain English, with Polish examples you can listen to, short exercises and flashcards that come back when you are about to forget them. Polish has seven cases, two aspects and a lot of endings: the map shows you which ones to learn first, and why.\n\n## How to start\n\n- **Not sure where you are?** [Take the placement test](#/placement): 12 questions, about 6 minutes. It tells you your level and where to start on the trail.\n- **New to Polish?** [Start the trail at A1](#/trail): the first stop is greetings, the alphabet and how to say who you are.\n- **Just want to look around?** Open a level below. Nothing needs an account.\n\n## Levels at a glance\n\n| Level | Land | Goal | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | Meadow | Introduce yourself and handle the simplest everyday situations when people speak slowly and clearly. | Available |\n| A2 | Forest | Get by in everyday situations: travel, shopping, plans, and simple stories about the past. | Coming soon |\n| B1 | Hills | Speak in connected sentences about experiences, plans and opinions, and cope with most situations while travelling. | Coming soon |\n| B2 | Lake | Talk fluently and in detail with native speakers, argue a point and understand the main ideas of complex texts. | Coming soon |\n| C1 | Mountains | Express yourself flexibly and precisely in social, academic and professional settings. | Coming soon |\n| C2 | Summit | Understand virtually everything and express fine shades of meaning effortlessly, even in complex situations. | Coming soon |\n\nLevels marked \"Coming soon\" already show their planned topics, so you can see where the trail goes next.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. **The trail.** Each level is a land, and each land is a path of stops: one stop per topic, with a short quiz, and a checkpoint at the end of each unit. Follow it in order, or jump to any topic from the level page.\n2. **\"I know this\".** Tick a topic when you are confident. Your level page and the map fill in as you go, and the final test of the level checks that it holds.\n3. **Review.** Wrong answers go to your mistake log, and words you save go to My words. Both come back in short review sessions, spaced out so that each one arrives just before you would forget it. Today shows what is due.\n4. **The Case Lab.** All seven cases on one map: what each one does, its endings, a \"Pick the case\" helper and every form of a phrase side by side.\n\n## Your data\n\nNo accounts, no tracking: your progress is stored only in this browser. Export a backup file from the profile menu if you want to move to another device or keep a copy. Clearing your browser data deletes your progress.\n\n## Found a mistake?\n\nEvery topic page, set result and flashcard has a quiet **Report a mistake** link. It opens a new report (an issue on GitHub) with the page already named: say what looks wrong and, if you can, what it should say. Every report is read and every confirmed mistake is fixed.\n"};
